[{:db/id "q-128190/field-1/51797dc60c7430836b8dc2862a4c73304c3890dbfe69a06d55082f896db71ff5" :answer/id #uuid "7a638abc-8e14-5f91-b75a-0b84c2419d43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\pi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128190/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d362fcc6-990b-514b-a399-18cb71a04a51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128190/field-1/51797dc60c7430836b8dc2862a4c73304c3890dbfe69a06d55082f896db71ff5" :answer-field/choices ["q-128190/field-1/51797dc60c7430836b8dc2862a4c73304c3890dbfe69a06d55082f896db71ff5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128190" :question/id #uuid "4319fedf-6b51-5c46-9fbb-4b3a1620cef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128190" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nUse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{93}{16}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & =\\frac{2-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[y(x)-2]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{y}^{′}(x)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(1)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (y(2))^{2}}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128190/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128190"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128191/field-1/650d7e10185e0155e97a4644adf1386133e82e86d5f96d57245147be10e5ccde" :answer/id #uuid "4679d4e8-2189-5156-bfa9-854bb936c71f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128191/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d1ca0d-c3f4-5084-88bd-6eb3d968da3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128191/field-1/650d7e10185e0155e97a4644adf1386133e82e86d5f96d57245147be10e5ccde" :answer-field/choices ["q-128191/field-1/650d7e10185e0155e97a4644adf1386133e82e86d5f96d57245147be10e5ccde"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128191" :question/id #uuid "b8214130-3851-56ee-8760-b9c737ba1f25" :question/math-academy-id "q-128191" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nUse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{93}{16}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{4}{\\pi}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}.$ Assume that $y\\ne 0$ and $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y=-2($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+K)^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2C}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+2C}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+2C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+K} \\\\ y & =-2{({x}^{2}+K)}^{-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128191/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128191"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128186/field-1/d9571e89d0504e1e2cf2b622aa85e4b9404697061234af459495e2b40a0c2074" :answer/id #uuid "1af518df-8f78-5cc7-97bc-d898e1518214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128186/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac4ca70e-93df-5be1-85b9-e0b0a00bb3ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128186/field-1/d9571e89d0504e1e2cf2b622aa85e4b9404697061234af459495e2b40a0c2074" :answer-field/choices ["q-128186/field-1/d9571e89d0504e1e2cf2b622aa85e4b9404697061234af459495e2b40a0c2074"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128186" :question/id #uuid "12488200-8812-5766-b560-cf163c2c7931" :question/math-academy-id "q-128186" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=x{y}^{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=1\\cdot {2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+4(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-128186/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128186"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128187/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "d02f5d47-cfb9-5678-97ec-290c55045762" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128187/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "863ad384-cfaf-52fa-ba0f-4910d6b29f71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128187/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-128187/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128187" :question/id #uuid "33d5c266-bf78-572d-9df3-496b3a4053f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-128187" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-128187/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128187"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128188/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "ec42d512-9e1f-542a-9b8c-0c51c88edd02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128188/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ab55a1-1101-5761-b93a-cfaab1596e3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :an