                 \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $c=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-128188/field-1" "q-128188/field-2" "q-128188/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128188"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128192/field-1/bf9d3b5efb5a6e816848933d8f29cb2ff13ad3fd21c2069637b80c6ed6c8b2a9" :answer/id #uuid "0a2d691a-650a-5683-a793-292253223f72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2-x^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128192/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2c065c2-23ab-5f7e-878b-0adb46dec64b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128192/field-1/bf9d3b5efb5a6e816848933d8f29cb2ff13ad3fd21c2069637b80c6ed6c8b2a9" :answer-field/choices ["q-128192/field-1/bf9d3b5efb5a6e816848933d8f29cb2ff13ad3fd21c2069637b80c6ed6c8b2a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128192" :question/id #uuid "756d0802-6ca6-5052-9d15-93cd1273b68c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128192" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nUse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{93}{16}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{4}{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}.$ Assume that $y\\ne 0$ and $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y=-2($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $x^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+K)^{-1}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(x)=\\,\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From part 6, we found the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{({x}^{2}+K)}^{-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=2$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -2{({1}^{2}+K)}^{-1} & =2 \\\\ 1+K & =-1 \\\\ K & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2({x}^{2}-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128192/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128192"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128189/field-1/175a9eea030bb78b6c7473aaaa0a5076008f911fb1ef134a9cdb77bcdc70c2af" :answer/id #uuid "6adc2c77-fb70-5365-a69f-8f631dfade04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{93}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128189/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "534149f2-32f6-5ac9-9537-0a84785adf55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128189/field-1/175a9eea030bb78b6c7473aaaa0a5076008f911fb1ef134a9cdb77bcdc70c2af" :answer-field/choices ["q-128189/field-1/175a9eea030bb78b6c7473aaaa0a5076008f911fb1ef134a9cdb77bcdc70c2af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128189" :question/id #uuid "7e7dc22a-fba5-58de-bc87-97e5868d8906" :question/math-academy-id "q-128189" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x-2$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWe wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nUse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll start with the table from part 3.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now proceed to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using Euler's method.\n\n**Step 1.** We compute the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{45}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{45}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{45}{16} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+\\frac{45}{16}=\\frac{93}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{45}{4}$ | $\\frac{45}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{93}{16}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{93}{16}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["